To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in Next.js while using WordPress, you can follow these steps:

1. Install and configure the `cors` package in your Next.js project. You can use npm or yarn to install it:
```bash
npm install --save cors
```
or
```bash
yarn add cors
```
2. Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages/api` directory. This file will handle the CORS requests.
3. Add the following code to the `cors.js` file:
```javascript
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Content-Type', 'Authorization'],
  origin: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com', // Replace with the URL of your WordPress site
});

export default cors.handle;
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the URL of your WordPress site.
4. In your Next.js component where you're making the request to WordPress, import the `useCors` hook from the `cors` package and wrap your fetch or axios call with it. For example:
```javascript
import { useCors } from 'cors';

const MyComponent = () => {
  const { cors } = useCors();

  const fetchData = async () => {
    try {
      const response = await fetch('https://your-wordpress-site.com/api/data', {
        method: 'GET',
        headers: {
          'Content-Type': 'application/json',
          'Authorization': 'Bearer YOUR_AUTHORIZATION_TOKEN', // Replace with your authorization token
        },
      });

      const data = await response.json();
      console.log(data);
    } catch (error) {
      console.error(error);
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={fetchData}>Fetch Data</button>
    </div>
  );
};

