Here's an example of a Mosquito MQTT configuration file that is well-suited for accepting connections from IoT devices:

```
{
  "listener": {
    "type": "tcp",
    "port": 1883,
    "backlog": 1024,
    "keepalive": 60,
    "cleansession": true
  },
  "topic": {
    "max_qos": 2,
    "retained": true,
    "last_will": {
      "topic": "$SYS/broker/heartbeat",
      "message": "Broker is alive",
      "qos": 0,
      "retain": false
    }
  },
  "client": {
    "max_connections": 100,
    "max_reconnect_attempts": 5,
    "reconnect_interval": 1000,
    "clean_session": true
  },
  "security": {
    "enabled": false,
    "certificate": "/path/to/certificate.pem",
    "key": "/path/to/key.pem"
  },
  "logger": {
    "level": "debug",
    "file": "/path/to/mosquitto.log"
  }
}
```

This configuration file sets up a TCP listener on port 1883 with a backlog of 1024 connections and a keepalive interval of 60 seconds. It also enables cleansession and sets the maximum QoS to 2. The topic settings ensure that retained messages are stored and that a last will message is sent in case the broker disconnects. The client settings limit the number of connections to 100 and the number of reconnect attempts to 5. The security settings disable TLS/SSL encryption, but you can enable it and specify the certificate and key files if needed. Finally, the logger settings set the log level to debug and output the logs to a file named mosquitto.log.

