To retrieve the username from the JWT token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens package if you haven't already. You can do this by running the following command in the Package Manager Console:
```
Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens
```
2. In your WebApi project, create a new class that will handle the JWT token validation and retrieval of the username. For example:
```csharp
using System;
using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
using System.Security.Claims;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;

public class JwtTokenHandler
{
    private readonly TokenValidationParameters _tokenValidationParameters;

    public JwtTokenHandler(string key)
    {
        _tokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            ValidateIssuer = true,
            ValidateAudience = true,
            IssuerSigningKey = key,
            ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero
        };
    }

    public string GetUsername(string token)
    {
        var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
        var securityToken = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token, _tokenValidationParameters);
        var usernameClaim = securityToken.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == "username");
        return usernameClaim?.Value;
    }
}
```
3. In your WebApi controller, you can use the `JwtTokenHandler` to retrieve the username from the JWT token. For example:
```csharp
using System;
using System.Web.Mvc;
using YourProjectName.Models;

public class HomeController : Controller
{
    private readonly JwtTokenHandler _jwtTokenHandler;

    public HomeController(JwtTokenHandler jwtTokenHandler)
    {
        _jwtTokenHandler = jwtTokenHandler;
    }

    public ActionResult Index()
    {
        string token = // Retrieve the JWT token from the request or session
        string username = _jwtTokenHandler.GetUsername(token);
        ViewBag.Username = username;
        return View();
    }
}

Inner and outer functions are types of functions in programming, specifically in languages that support function nesting. Inner functions are defined within another function, while outer functions are defined at the global level or outside of any other function. Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```javascript
// Outer function
function greet(name) {
  console.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);
}

// Inner function
function greetInner() {
  console.log(`Greetings from inside the greet function!`);
}

greet("John"); // Output: Hello, John!
greetInner(); // Output: Greetings from inside the greet function!
```

In this example, `greet` is an outer function that takes a name parameter and prints a greeting message. `greetInner` is an inner function that is defined within the `greet` function and prints a different message. When we call `greet`, it prints "Hello, John!" as expected. However, when we call `greetInner`, it prints "Greetings from inside the greet function!" because it is executed within the context of the `greet` function.

Inner functions can be useful for organizing code and improving readability, especially when working with complex functions or multiple levels of function calls. They also allow for the creation of closures, which are functions that have access to the variables and context of their surrounding function, even after that function has returned.

Overall, inner and outer functions are important concepts in programming, and understanding how they work can help you write more efficient and maintainable code.

