To call a PowerShell script from Terraform and allow a Read-Host input, you can use the `local-exec` provisioner in Terraform. Here's an example:

1. Create a PowerShell script (e.g., `read_host_input.ps1`) with the following content:
```powershell
# read-host input
$input = Read-Host "Enter some input"

# output the input
Write-Host "The input is: $input"
```
1. In your Terraform configuration file (e.g., `main.tf`), add a `local-exec` provisioner to call the PowerShell script:
```hcl
provider "null" {
  version = "3.0.0"
}

resource "null_resource" "read_host_input" {
  triggers = {
    always = "${timestamp()}"
  }

  connection {
    type        = "ssh"
    user        = "your_username"
    private_key = "${file("~/.ssh/your_private_key")}"
    host        = "${var.server_ip}"
    timeout     = "2m"
  }

  provisioner "local-exec" {
    command = "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File read_host_input.ps1"
  }
}
```
Replace `your_username`, `your_private_key`, and `your_server_ip` with the appropriate values for your environment.

When you apply this configuration, Terraform will execute the PowerShell script on the specified server and prompt for input using the `Read-Host` cmdlet. The output of the script will be displayed in the Terraform console.

