To create a React utility that removes widows, you'll need to use the `React` and `ReactDOM` libraries. Here's a simple example of how you can create such a utility:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';

const WidowRemover = ({ children }) => {
  const [widowedText, setWidowedText] = React.useState('');

  const handleTextChange = (event) => {
    const text = event.target.value;
    const words = text.split(' ');
    const lastWord = words[words.length - 1];

    if (lastWord !== '') {
      setWidowedText(lastWord);
    } else {
      setWidowedText('');
    }
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <textarea value={children} onChange={handleTextChange} />
      <p>{widowedText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

ReactDOM.render(<WidowRemover>Your text here</WidowRemover>, document.getElementById('root'));
```

This component will display a text area where you can enter your text, and it will display the last word of the text in a paragraph below it. You can use this component to identify and remove widows from your text.

Note that this is a simple example and may not be suitable for large-scale applications. For more advanced widow removal functionality, you might want to consider using a library like `react-widow-remover` or `react-text-widow-remover`.

