Here's an example of how you can create a socket for a chatroom using the Socket.IO library in Node.js:

```javascript
const http = require('http');
const socketio = require('socket.io');

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  res.statusCode = 200;
  res.setHeader('Content-Type', 'text/plain');
  res.end('Hello World');
});

const io = socketio(server);

server.listen(3000, () => {
  console.log('Server listening on port 3000');
});

io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  console.log('User connected');

  // Handle chat messages
  socket.on('chat message', (message) => {
    console.log(`Received message from ${socket.id}: ${message}`);
    io.emit('chat message', message);
  });

  // Handle user disconnection
  socket.on('disconnect', () => {
    console.log('User disconnected');
  });
});
```

This code sets up an HTTP server and creates a Socket.IO instance on it. When a user connects to the server, the server emits a 'connection' event, which can be handled by the server to perform any initial setup or configuration.

The server also listens for 'chat message' events, which are sent by clients when they send a message. When a message is received, the server broadcasts it to all connected clients using the 'emit' method.

Finally, the server handles 'disconnect' events, which are emitted by clients when they disconnect from the server. This allows the server to clean up any resources associated with the disconnected client.

To use this code in your project, you'll need to install the required dependencies using npm:

```bash
npm install http socket.io
```

